eally have much of an impact  still until you get to reduce and  conventional but planting - we did have  a significant decrease in crop I'm sorry  and weed biomass from the tarps so this  may just be a seasonal variation this  may be because many of the weeds had  already emerged for the planting - and  so when we actually put the tarps down  for planting - the shorter duration  tarps were going down on already emerged  weeds and so that killed everything that  was already there this is a graph from  Riverhead however so as you see here  this is also 10 days later and there was  still almost 100% control of weeds ten  days later as opposed to in freebo so  because of these contrasting results we  wanted to look at the weed species  dynamics so here I have the top five to  six weed species for each site that we  had and I put a start by some of these  because these are the ones that had some  interesting dynamics so first of all  pigweed was extremely prominent and free  though  and in Mon mouth and we actually found  in some treatments that pigweed  increased under tarps this has also been  found in studies with plastic mulch  pigweed is a hoarder of nitrogen so if  you increase nitrate that much for the  tarps especially with the added benefits  of the temperature and the moisture it's  likely to increase in germination red  clover also has a star by it because  this in Mon mouth they had a huge red  clover population it was entirely  decimated by crops here by tarps so  tarps clearly have very species specific  effects for example in free bill common  chickweed was by far the top species in  terms of prevalence but it was also very  well controlled by tarps so when using  tarps on farms you really have to know  what species you have and the tarp  impact on your weed suppression will  depend on what species you have on your  farm so when we started to look at the  interaction between tarps and tillage we  found a very interesting interaction so  this graph is gram